{"version": "1.0", "event_type": "command", "source_id": "openwebui-gateway", "correlation_id": "fc9eb3e4-7622-4e14-bb61-2e33fd6225b2", "payload": {"command": "chat", "args": {"message": "### Task:\nSuggest 3-5 relevant follow-up questions or prompts that the user might naturally ask next in this conversation as a **user**, based on the chat history, to help continue or deepen the discussion.\n### Guidelines:\n- Write all follow-up questions from the user\u2019s point of view, directed to the assistant.\n- Make questions concise, clear, and directly related to the discussed topic(s).\n- Only suggest follow-ups that make sense given the chat content and do not repeat what was already covered.\n- If the conversation is very short or not specific, suggest more general (but relevant) follow-ups the user might ask.\n- Use the conversation's primary language; default to English if multilingual.\n- Response must be a JSON object with a \"follow_ups\" key containing an array of strings, no extra text or formatting.\n### Output:\nJSON format: { \"follow_ups\": [\"Question 1?\", \"Question 2?\", \"Question 3?\"] }\n### Chat History:\n<chat_history>\nUSER: What agents do you have available, and can you ask the architect to evaluate whether ADR-ARCH-001's local-first invariant should allow a budget-capped cloud escalation path?\nASSISTANT: The architect agent isn't online in the fleet right now. Here's the situation:\n\n**Available agents in the fleet:**\n- **Product Owner Agent** \u2014 registered and available (tools: `po_idea`, `po_extract`, `po_greenfield`, `po_evolve`, `po_impact`, `po_scope`, etc.)\n- **Architect Agent** \u2014 *not currently registered* in the fleet\n- **Ideation Agent** \u2014 *not currently registered*\n- **Forge** \u2014 *not currently registered*\n\nThe architect capability listed in my session prompt is offline. You can bring it back with:\n\n```\ncapabilities_refresh\n```\n\n\u2014or have the architect come online and then tell me \"check again.\"\n\n**In the meantime**, I can still give you my take on the ADR-ARCH-001 question. Here it is:\n\n---\n\n**Yes, a budget-capped cloud escalation path is compatible with local-first.** Here's why:\n\n1. **Local-first means default, not absolute.** A budget cap + explicit consent preserves the invariant \u2014 the system *prefers* local but has a controlled escape hatch. The invariant is about direction, not rigidity.\n\n2. **Safeguards that make this work:**\n   - **Explicit user opt-in** per escalation (no silent cloud calls)\n   - **Per-request budget cap** (e.g., \u00a30.50/request) so costs are predictable\n   - **Audit log** of every escalation (what was sent, cost, model used)\n   - **Monthly hard cap** (\u00a320-50) with auto-shutoff when reached\n   - **Data scoping** \u2014 only the minimal context needed, not the full conversation\n\n3. **The real risk isn't the escape hatch \u2014 it's scope creep.** Without tight controls, \"occasional cloud use\" becomes the default and local-first becomes local-when-convenient. The budget cap alone isn't enough; you need the consent gate and audit trail.\n\n4. **Operational benefit:** For frontier-tier questions (complex reasoning, code generation on unfamiliar codebases), local 120B models genuinely can't compete. Forcing local-only on those tasks degrades output quality, which undermines the whole system's utility.\n\n**Bottom line:** The invariant should say \"local unless the user explicitly opts into cloud, with strict per-request and monthly budget caps and full auditability.\" That's local-first with teeth, not local-first with an open door.\n\nWant me to bring the architect online to get a formal ADR review on this?\nUSER: capabilities_refresh\nASSISTANT: Architect is back online and has weighed in. Here's the summary:\n\n**Verdict: Conditionally feasible** (confidence: 85%)\n\n**Key findings:**\n- The escalation path is technically achievable if implemented as an **opt-in mechanism**, not a default \u2014 preserving the local-first principle.\n- The real challenge isn't connectivity; it's the safeguards.\n\n**Risks flagged:**\n1. **Data leakage** \u2014 sensitive data leaving premises without explicit consent\n2. **Budget overrun** \u2014 per-request caps not enforced at the gateway\n3. **Operational complexity** \u2014 managing dual-mode routing (local vs. cloud)\n\n**Recommendations:**\n- **Explicit consent gate** \u2014 user must confirm before any data leaves premises\n- **BudgetController component** \u2014 enforce the \u00a320-50 monthly cap at the infrastructure layer\n- **Escalate only when local fails** \u2014 only route to cloud when local models can't meet quality thresholds\n- **Audit logging** \u2014 track every escalation event (cost, data movement, model used)\n\n**Estimated complexity:** Medium \u2014 requires new budget enforcement and consent components.\n\nSo the architect agrees with my assessment in principle: the invariant should allow escalation, but only with tight controls. The key addition from the architect is the **BudgetController** as a dedicated infrastructure component \u2014 that's a concrete design element worth capturing if we draft an ADR update.\n\nWant to proceed with drafting an ADR amendment, or is there something else you'd like to explore?\nUSER: Of those agents, which one would you escalate to if a runtime quality gate failed during an autobuild turn? Walk me through how that handoff would work.\nASSISTANT: That's a good question, but none of the registered agents are designed for runtime quality-gate failures during autobuild. Let me walk through what each would actually do:\n\n**Architect** \u2014 Could assess whether the failure indicates a drift from documented architecture, but that's a *post-mortem* analysis, not a runtime intervention. It doesn't have the authority or mechanism to stop or redirect a build.\n\n**Product Owner** \u2014 Could evaluate whether the failure changes the priority or scope of the feature, but again, that's a planning-level concern. It doesn't handle runtime build failures.\n\n**Forge** \u2014 This is the build pipeline itself. The quality gate failure would surface *to* Forge as an event, not *through* another agent. Forge is where the build runs; it's the source of the failure signal, not the resolver.\n\n**Ideation** \u2014 Could brainstorm workarounds, but has no integration with the build system to apply them.\n\n**The honest answer:** There's no handoff because there's no agent designed for this. A runtime quality-gate failure is an **operational event**, not an architectural or product question. The current agent fleet treats builds as fire-and-forget (queue and wait for notifications).\n\n**What would actually need to happen:**\n\n1. The quality gate failure emits an event (e.g., `pipeline.build-stage-failed.{feature_id}`)\n2. Jarvis receives it and assesses severity\n3. If it's a transient failure (flaky test, infra issue) \u2192 retry\n4. If it's a real defect \u2192 **stop the build**, notify the user, and *then* potentially route to the right agent:\n   - **Architect** if the failure suggests an architectural contradiction\n   - **Product Owner** if the failure changes acceptance criteria or scope\n   - **Ideation** if we need alternative approaches to unblock\n\nBut that's a **new capability** \u2014 a build incident responder \u2014 that doesn't exist in the current fleet. The closest thing is Jarvis itself handling it, since I'm the general-purpose agent and the event lands in my context.\n\nWant me to flag this as a gap? It feels like there should be a `forge_monitor` or `build_responder` capability that sits between the pipeline events and the specialist agents, deciding which path to take when things go wrong.\n</chat_history>", "conversation_history": [{"role": "user", "content": "### Task:\nSuggest 3-5 relevant follow-up questions or prompts that the user might naturally ask next in this conversation as a **user**, based on the chat history, to help continue or deepen the discussion.\n### Guidelines:\n- Write all follow-up questions from the user\u2019s point of view, directed to the assistant.\n- Make questions concise, clear, and directly related to the discussed topic(s).\n- Only suggest follow-ups that make sense given the chat content and do not repeat what was already covered.\n- If the conversation is very short or not specific, suggest more general (but relevant) follow-ups the user might ask.\n- Use the conversation's primary language; default to English if multilingual.\n- Response must be a JSON object with a \"follow_ups\" key containing an array of strings, no extra text or formatting.\n### Output:\nJSON format: { \"follow_ups\": [\"Question 1?\", \"Question 2?\", \"Question 3?\"] }\n### Chat History:\n<chat_history>\nUSER: What agents do you have available, and can you ask the architect to evaluate whether ADR-ARCH-001's local-first invariant should allow a budget-capped cloud escalation path?\nASSISTANT: The architect agent isn't online in the fleet right now. Here's the situation:\n\n**Available agents in the fleet:**\n- **Product Owner Agent** \u2014 registered and available (tools: `po_idea`, `po_extract`, `po_greenfield`, `po_evolve`, `po_impact`, `po_scope`, etc.)\n- **Architect Agent** \u2014 *not currently registered* in the fleet\n- **Ideation Agent** \u2014 *not currently registered*\n- **Forge** \u2014 *not currently registered*\n\nThe architect capability listed in my session prompt is offline. You can bring it back with:\n\n```\ncapabilities_refresh\n```\n\n\u2014or have the architect come online and then tell me \"check again.\"\n\n**In the meantime**, I can still give you my take on the ADR-ARCH-001 question. Here it is:\n\n---\n\n**Yes, a budget-capped cloud escalation path is compatible with local-first.** Here's why:\n\n1. **Local-first means default, not absolute.** A budget cap + explicit consent preserves the invariant \u2014 the system *prefers* local but has a cont